#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "07394b09-ab80-579c-9b10-dc7a2835d3c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6595" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 25y =0$." :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} + 25e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} + 25) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 25 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 25 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -25 \\\\\nλ &= ± 5\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny = P\\cos{5x} + Q\\sin{5x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087535 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "b22fa1be-4c82-5dbf-b5fe-5cbb42c0c646" :question/math-academy-id "e-6596" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y'' - 4y' + 85y = 0$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $-2e^{2t}\\cos{9t} + 7e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$\n2. $e^{2t}\\cos{9t}+3e^{2t}\\sin{3t}$\n3. $-6e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny = e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} - 4λe^{λt} + 85e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} - 4λ + 85) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 85 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 4λ + 4) - 4 + 85 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} + 81 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} &= -81 \\\\\nλ - 2 &= ± 9\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 2 ± 9\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nare the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left(P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t$, which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa1ff4a-2263-5c99-97dd-d6fcada8bfc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6597" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y'' + 10y' + 34y = 0$, where $y = y(t)$. If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda$?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny = e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} + 10λe^{λt} + 34e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} + 10λ + 34) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 34 = 0\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 10λ + 34 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 10λ + 25) - 25 + 34 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 5)^{2} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 5)^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nλ + 5 &= ± 3\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -5 ± 3\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-5 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nare the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "04253a00-f441-58d8-b97e-f7935ae5c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6598" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 4 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 20y =0$." :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our ODE gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} - 4λe^{λx} + 20e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} - 4λ + 20) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 20 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 4λ + 4) - 4 + 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} &= -16 \\\\\nλ - 2 &= ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 2 ± 4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= e^{2x}(P\\cos 4x + Q\\sin 4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a647d8e4-f1fc-5a09-b1c2-bada25e86faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 ± \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085988} {:answer/id #uuid "aeba4de2-1ea5-5793-85a3-71a0db65aaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085989} {:answer/id #uuid "06574644-cc51-525d-8674-864381218855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085990} {:answer/id #uuid "7733575a-c92c-537e-9a40-623990375fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085991} {:answer/id #uuid "7a44f641-a446-593c-a903-d45cca5d69c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 ± \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a647d8e4-f1fc-5a09-b1c2-bada25e86faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 ± \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085988} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f82137-fd17-5dff-a7a4-24b5296fb670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085987}] :question/id #uuid "70c30108-c302-53ad-8b6c-df208afe3a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-64663" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 10y = 0$ where $y = y(t)$. If $y = e^{λt}$ is a solution, then $λ =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c39489-5f70-52b0-a3bb-f8679260a4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\t